I went to my first a a meeting when I  was 7 years old and I know what you guys  are thinking what the hell within this  girl's baby bottle spoiler alert I was  not an alcoholic at age 7 my uncle took  me to that AAA meeting because there  were people in my family who were in  fact there are people in my family  suffering from many forms of addiction  knowing that addiction would  dramatically affect my life he wanted me  to be informed about it so he exposed me  to addiction from the viewpoint of an  addict so that I could understand their  struggle according to the center of  addiction 40 million Americans are  suffering from substance addiction this  includes alcohol illicit drugs and  prescription opioids 40 million that's  nearly one in every eight people look at  your row on average at least one person  is addicted 138 million Americans have a  close friend or family member who is  suffering from addiction this nearly 50%  of our entire country who knows an  addict and yet due to shame people that  know an addict never talk about it  they hide it from their friends they  hide it from their neighbors they even  hide it from other family members people  who know an addict go through great  lights to keep this secret  I know because up until this talk I was  one of these people drug addiction is  defined by the National Institute for  drug abuse as a complex chronic brain  disease characterized by drug craving  seeking and youth the key to this  craving is dopamine dopamine is a  neurotransmitter in your brain that's  responsible for pleasure totally natural  and necessary  in dopamine surges happen when you're  watching a movie you really love or  eating foods that you enjoy perhaps  chocolate or pasta or when you're having  sex well hopefully when you consume a  drug over time it's over stimulates the  dopamine in your system causing a  euphoric effect that is 2 to 10 times  the amount that your body is used to  this is the high but over time when you  continue to consume drugs it the surges  in dopamine really make it difficult to  participate in life fully this is why  when addicts aren't using drugs they  complain of feeling flat lifeless and  depressed all the time at this point it  stops being about using drugs to get  high and starts being about using drugs  just to feel normal just to feel  something an addict needs substances to  reach the same levels of dopamine as a  non drug user  it literally addiction literally rewires  your brain addiction is not a choice  this is literally a brain disease so the  question then becomes if research has  proven that addiction is not a choice  but a brain disease how in the United  States do we justify putting addicts and  treatment in prisons instead of  treatment centers I mean would we put  cancer patients in prison because they  have cancer of course not 65% of prison  inmates are clinically addicted many of  them were convicted for personal drug  use not even intent to sell  we put addicts in prison for the  compulsive use of a drug that their  brain literally tells them that they  need to survive but this is not just  about an increase in the  and population addiction cost our  economy a lot of money five hundred and  twenty billion dollars to be exact this  is the cost to our economy and lost work  productivity health care expenses  criminal justice costs and law  enforcement costs and the problem is  only growing opioid addiction alone has  grown by four hundred and ninety three  percent in the last seven years the US  Surgeon General has declared opioid  addiction a public health crisis that is  both underappreciated and undertreated  do addicts choose to try these  substances yes  were they coerced into trying them  probably not but does that mean that  they choose an entire life of addiction  no no one grows up and says I really  really want to be an addict when I get  older they just don't I spoken to you  guys today about a number of statistics  but addiction is not statistics  addiction is the mother who lies to her  family and friends because she is too  ashamed to admit that her son has a drug  problem addiction is the father who  steals from his kids piggy banks to feed  his drug habit  addiction is being called by the police  to identify your sister's body because  she just overdosed these are not  examples that I made up these are  examples from people that I know some of  whom are in this room right now my uncle  took me to that a a meeting when I was  seven because he knew what I did not  know my mother was addicted to crack  like many AD  she was unreliable she was irresponsible  and when I was four she stopped caring  for me altogether growing up as the  child of an addict is a very painful and  traumatic experience you live in secrecy  mortified that people will judge you for  this disease so you do everything you  can to keep it hidden when I was little  I used to just think if I could just get  more straight A's or more Awards she  would want to be my mom again I didn't  understand how my mom could have won a  drug more than she wanted me  I recently remit my mom after 23 years  people asked me how do you forgive an  addict how do you forgive addiction  understanding that this is not a choice  but literally a brain disease is a  critical step I wasn't sure how to give  this talk today because the topic is so  taboo and so personal but there are  literally 40 million Americans that are  suffering from this disease and if we do  not start treating this like the health  issue that it really is we are further  enabling the disease that is killing  thousands destroying families all over  this country and has a real economic  cost we as a society have to make a  paradigm shift into how we are  addressing addiction one that creates  more patients and fewer prisoners thank  you  [Applause]  [Music]
 

